  <section>
    <h2>Why Stripe, Square, SumUp and Zettle refuse CBD</h2>
    <p>The four mainstream UK card processors — Stripe, Square, SumUp and Zettle — explicitly list CBD on their prohibited-business pages. The reasons are policy, not legality: their underwriting models are tuned for low-touch onboarding and global uniformity, and a product that is legal in the UK but federally illegal in the US (where Stripe and Square are headquartered) creates a compliance burden their flat-rate economics can't absorb.</p>
    <p>Practically, this means a UK CBD retailer who signs up to Stripe or Square typically processes for a few weeks, then has the account frozen and funds held when the underwriting team reviews the MCC. The resolution involves a long disclosure process and almost always ends in account closure with a 90–180 day reserve hold. The cleaner route is to start on a CBD-friendly acquirer from day one — see the <a href="/high-risk/">UK high-risk hub</a> for the full pattern across MCCs.</p>
    <p>Sister verticals see the same pattern: <a href="/high-risk/vape/">vape</a> and <a href="/high-risk/nutraceuticals/">nutraceuticals</a> are refused for the same compliance-burden reason, even though both are fully legal in the UK.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>UK CBD legality — the FSA novel-foods refresher</h2>
    <p>CBD is legal to sell in the UK provided the product contains no controlled cannabinoids — in practice, no THC at all in finished retail products, with a 1mg-per-container limit applied as a regulatory safe harbour. The Misuse of Drugs Regulations 2001 govern the THC question; the Food Standards Agency (FSA) governs the food and supplement question through its novel-foods regime.</p>
    <p>Since 2020 the FSA has required all ingestible CBD products on UK shelves to be on its novel-foods public list, either with full authorisation or with a validated application in progress. Acquirers underwriting CBD will check this list directly when reviewing your application — products that aren't listed (or whose application has lapsed) are an instant decline regardless of how clean the rest of the file looks.</p>
    <p>Topicals (balms, creams, cosmetics) sit outside novel foods but are governed by the cosmetics regulations and still need MHRA-aware claims. Vapes containing CBD straddle both regimes and are the hardest CBD product to underwrite — most acquirers will accept the rest of your range and ask you to drop CBD vape SKUs.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>UK acquirers that do accept CBD</h2>
    <p>The UK panel of acquirers that consistently underwrite CBD is small but real. The main three are <strong>Trust Payments</strong> (the broadest CBD appetite, accepts ingestibles, topicals and most vape categories with documentation), <strong>Universe Payments</strong> (accepts CBD with a tilt towards larger merchants and online-led models), and <strong>Acquired.com</strong> (UK-licensed, strong on subscription-billing CBD brands and recurring boxes).</p>
    <p>Outside the core three, <strong>PurePay</strong> and the <strong>Worldpay high-risk team</strong> will quote on larger CBD merchants — Worldpay's high-risk underwriting line is separate from the mainstream Worldpay product, so a previous decline on the standard product does not carry across. Each acquirer has slightly different appetite by sub-category: ingestibles are the cleanest, topicals next, and CBD vape the most restricted.</p>
    <p>The key buyer move is to apply to two or three of the panel in parallel rather than sequentially — the underwriting timelines are long enough that a single rejection costs you a month. See the <a href="/high-risk/why-declined/">why-declined guide</a> for the application-package mistakes that cause avoidable rejections.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Underwriting documents you'll need</h2>
    <p>Expect the underwriting pack for a UK CBD merchant account to be deeper than a mainstream application. The non-negotiables: certificates of analysis (COAs) from a UKAS-accredited lab for every SKU, showing cannabinoid content (THC at non-detectable levels) and absence of heavy metals, pesticides and microbial contaminants. COAs must match batch numbers on your inventory; recycled or supplier-generic COAs are a common reason for decline.</p>
    <p>You will also need: FSA novel-foods application reference numbers for every ingestible SKU; product label photographs (front and back) showing dosage, ingredients and statutory warnings; a website URL with claims that don't stray into medicinal territory (no "treats", "cures", "anxiety", "pain"); company KYC (Companies House extract, directors' ID and proof of address); 3–6 months of business bank statements; and projected monthly volume with an average transaction value.</p>
    <p>Subscription / recurring CBD merchants need to add a churn-rate disclosure and a chargeback management policy. Online-only merchants need to add 3D Secure proof and a fraud-screening tool (Sift, Kount or the acquirer's own).</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Rolling reserves — what's typical for CBD</h2>
    <p>A rolling reserve is a percentage of your monthly card volume that the acquirer holds back as protection against chargebacks. For UK CBD, expect a rolling reserve of <strong>5–15% of monthly volume held for 90–180 days</strong>. Where you land in that range depends on volume, online vs in-store mix, and chargeback history.</p>
    <p>An established bricks-and-mortar CBD retailer with low chargeback history will land at the 5% / 90-day end. An online-led subscription CBD brand without trading history will land at the 15% / 180-day end, sometimes with an upfront security deposit on top. After 6–12 months of clean processing, every acquirer on the panel will renegotiate the reserve down — schedule the review proactively rather than waiting for the acquirer to offer it.</p>
    <p>Processing fees on top of the reserve typically run 2.5–3.9% per transaction for card-present and 3.5–4.9% for card-not-present, plus a monthly gateway fee. That's 2–4x mainstream rates, which is the price of the acquirer accepting the MCC at all.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Recommended terminal hardware for CBD shops</h2>
    <p>The hardware question is downstream of the acquirer choice — most high-risk acquirers will provision a PAX A920 Pro, a Castles Tech S1F2 or an Ingenico Move/5000, all of which run their own gateway and integrate with EPOS systems. You typically can't bring a SumUp Air or Zettle Reader 2 to a high-risk acquirer because those readers are tied to their own processors.</p>
    <p>For bricks-and-mortar CBD shops, the PAX A920 Pro is the most common pick — it's a full Android terminal with built-in receipt printer, Wi-Fi/4G, and integration into EPOS systems like Epos Now. For online-only CBD merchants the question is gateway choice rather than hardware: Trust Payments' TRU Connect, Acquired.com's hosted-fields gateway, and Universe's gateway all integrate with WooCommerce, Shopify (via custom payment app) and Magento.</p>
    <p>If you also run a vape range alongside CBD, see the <a href="/high-risk/vape/">vape merchant account guide</a> — the underwriting overlap is significant and most acquirers will write a single MID covering both categories.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>How to apply — a realistic timeline</h2>
    <p>A clean UK CBD merchant account application takes <strong>2–4 weeks end-to-end</strong>, occasionally faster for an established merchant moving from a refused mainstream account. Week 1 is the application pack and KYC: company documents, COAs, FSA references, label photos and projections. Week 2 is underwriter review and clarification questions — expect 2–3 rounds of back-and-forth on COAs and label compliance.</p>
    <p>Week 3 is approval, MID issuance and gateway / hardware provisioning. Week 4 is integration: gateway plumbed into your website or EPOS, test transactions, settlement file format confirmed. The longest delays are almost always document-quality issues on the merchant side — wrong COA batch numbers, expired FSA references, or label claims that the underwriter flags as medicinal.</p>
    <p>If you've already been declined by a mainstream provider with funds held, see the <a href="/high-risk/why-declined/">why-declined guide</a> — the declined account doesn't damage your high-risk application, but you do need to disclose it.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="cta-callout">
    <h2>Get matched with a CBD-friendly UK acquirer</h2>
    <p>We're standing up a quote-matching tool that pairs your CBD business — by product mix, monthly volume, and online/in-store split — with the UK acquirers most likely to underwrite you.</p>
